<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chremonidean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 267–261 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chremonidean War (c. 267–261 BCE) was a conflict in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and their allies, backed by Ptolemy II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, tried to end Macedonian domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Named after the Athenian politician Chremonides, who proposed the alliance, it ended with the defeat of the Spartan king Areus I near Corinth and the surrender of Athens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> after a long siege.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chremonidean-war.jpg" alt="Chremonidean War" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T8612 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chremonidean_War_-_First_Stage_(267-265_BC).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war is poorly documented and known mostly from fragmentary accounts by the later historians Pausanias and Justin. Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> wanted their former independence back from Antigonid Macedonia. At the same time the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> were threatened by the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a>, so Ptolemy built an anti-Macedonian coalition among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> and courted Athens by supplying it with grain. In Athens the anti-Macedonian faction led by Chremonides, a Stoic statesman and general, took power and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, possibly as early as the autumn of 268 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first year brought only minor clashes, which generally went the coalition's way. The campaign of 266 BCE, in which a Ptolemaic fleet under Patroclus assisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, was indecisive. After that the war turned against the Greek cities. With their main ally beaten outside Corinth and too weak to face the Antigonids alone, the Athenians stayed behind their walls, hoping that the Ptolemies would send help before the siege began.</p><p>Ptolemy II could not mount a major expedition until after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> had been starved into surrender, in either 262 or 261 BCE. When the Ptolemies did send reinforcements, their fleet was defeated off Cos, probably in 261 BCE; the Battle of Cos also figures in accounts of the Second Syrian War, with a strong alternative date of 258 or 255 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Macedonian victory confirmed Antigonid control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> lost the last of the political independence it had kept from before the Hellenistic age. Historians regard the war as a key event of Hellenistic Greece, since it was the last credible attempt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> to regain their independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chremonidean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antigonus Gonatas defeated the Ptolemaic fleet off Cos, at a date still debated, and the <b>Chremonidean War</b> (c. 267–261 BCE) ended with the surrender of Athens and Macedonian garrisons in its harbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
